ED  - Davenport, Julia L.
TI  - Leave no footprint? - The ecological impact of personal leisure transport.
T2  - The effects of human transport on ecosystems: cars and planes, boats and trains. Proceedings of a seminar of the National Committee for biology 1st and 2nd April 2003.
M3  - Book chapter
M3  - Meeting paper
AB  - Mass tourism and increasing prosperity in developed countries has created a worldwide demand for personal leisure transport, from simple walking and swimming to modern phenomena such as snowboarding, SCUBA, jet skiing and paragliding. This review considers the ecological impact of land, water and aerial personal transport. Broadly speaking, serious impacts are associated with localised pressure of numbers of people indulging in these activities, and with the development of new technologies that have so far escaped the regulations associated with conventional mass transport mechanisms, or which have greatly enhanced human mobility in challenging environments. Walking and trekking cause vegetation and soil damage; they also disturb wildlife. Particularly severe impacts are associated with high altitudeclimbing and mountaineering expeditions that dump garbage, depleterhododendron (Rhododendron indicum) forests for fuelwood and henceof create localised climate change. Mountain biking appears to create similar ecological disturbance to walking, but has the potential to spread further into wilderness areas because of the greater speed of mountain bikers. Horse riding has a substantially greater impact to soil and vegetation than walking or cycling; it also transmits weeds and manures soils that are naturally nutrient poor. Cross-country skiing creates little impact upon the environment; certainly no more than walking or hiking. Alpine skiing and snowboarding are a different matter. Most ecological impact is caused by the forest clearances and soil erosion caused by the creation of snow resorts and pistes. However, disturbance created by the numbers of people taken into alpine habitats is itself significant, contributing to range shrinkage in montane birds and mammals. Off road vehicles cause substantial damage to soils, vegetation and biota in wilderness, desert and coastal areas. They create trails that fragment habitat and cause range depletion in mammals, some of them close to extinction. Particularly severe damage is done by snowmobiles which produce considerable hydrocarbon and noise pollution, as well as crushing sub-snow tunnels inhabited by small and medium-sized mammals. Swimming, surfing and snorkelling are relatively innocuous, but use of SCUBA (which greatly extends underwater time) has been implicated in serious physical damage to coral reef habitats as well as in localised ecological disturbance created by removal of organisms for food or as trophies. Motorboats create a number of problems for wildlife, ranging from erosion of riverbanks, through spreading of pest species, to propeller damage of marine mammals and turtles. Sailboats share some of these problems, but are generally less harmful to the environment. In contrast, jet skis are identified as a serious emerging problem as they have highly polluting engines, create much noise and reach extremely high speeds, thus causing blunt trauma impact to a variety of air-breathing vertebrates. Personal aerial transport is still relatively limited. Paragliders and hang gliders appear innocuous, but may disturb mammals, as they resemble predatory birds. Powered aircraft create noise disturbance to wildlife colonies, mainly birds. This review concludes with a short consideration of the environmental management challenges posed by reconciling rising consumer prosperity, individual freedom and the necessity of minimising ecological deterioration.
