TI  - CONCEPTUAL DESIGN OF UNDERGROUND LANDFILLS IN SINGAPORE
T2  - ADVANCES IN UNDERGROUND SPACE DEVELOPMENT
M3  - Proceedings Paper
CP  - 13th World Conference of Associated-Research-Centers-for-the-Urban-Underground-Space (ACUUS)
CL  - Singapore, SINGAPORE
AB  - Singapore is an island city state of 710 sq km, with a population of 5.1 million people. To meet multiple land use demands within a small land size. Singapore has made use of strategies like land reclamation, compact and high-density development, to expand and optimize the use of its limited land. Going underground is another promising option for Singapore to further expand its land and space capacity.As part of an Underground Rock Cavern (URC) usage feasibility study performed by SINTEF-Tritech-Multiconsult (STM) Consortium for the Singapore Government, the feasibility of creating rock caverns for landfill use was explored. Landfills above-ground require large areas and often imply problems with heavy traffic, noise, odor nuisance, rodents and birds. Placing them underground will solve some of these challenges. This paper describes the conceptual design of an underground landfill for the combustion residue from incineration plants.The design of the storage volume is based on silos, inspired from the mining industry and storage silos. The access system consists of vertical shafts and a number of tunnels. The order of excavating shafts, tunnels and silos will be described. As soon as a small number of silos are ready, the excavation of new silos and waste-filling in ready-made silos will run in parallel. This means use of the storage volume can start after a relatively short period of construction, giving quick "payback" for the investment. The design also allows for a large degree of flexibility in the excavation rate, making it possible to meet changing prognosis for further waste volume.The silo-design allows for utilization of gravity both in the excavating phase and in the operational phase and contributes to less use of resources and energy and thereby reduces the costs.Special topics like water tightening and handling of leachate and biogas will also he mentioned
