TI  - Scenarios for the acoustic environment in Flanders in 2010
T2  - URBAN TRANSPORT VII: URBAN TRANSPORT AND THE ENVIRONMENT IN THE 21ST CENTURY
M3  - Proceedings Paper
CP  - 7th International Conference on Urban Transport and the Environment for the 21st Century
CL  - LEMNOS, GREECE
AB  - The Flemish Environment Agency's report 'MIRA-S 2000' indicates how the quality of the environment might develop in time and which policy interventions may improve it. Three scenarios for the period 2000-2010 are defined: the BAU scenario (business as usual), BAU+ and the ESD scenario (environmentally sustainable development). The assessment of noise pollution in this report is based on 3 indicators: the percentage of the population in Flanders exposed to road traffic day time equivalent noise levels over 65 dB(A), the number of people exposed to aircraft noise (Ldn > 60 dB(A)) near Brussels-National airport, and the percentage of the population potentially highly annoyed by noise.The reduced sound emission of individual vehicles, caused by EU emission regulation and effective speed reduction (BAU), will counterbalance the increase in road traffic only temporarily, In 1996, 27% of the Flemish population was exposed to road traffic noise. The proposed objective (< 20%) will only be met in the ESD scenario, The rise in the number of people exposed to aircraft noise around Brussels-National airport (50178 people in 1998) is due to the strong growth of air traffic. According to the planned policy (BAU) this number will be less than 45000 people in 2003, due to the replacement of noisy aircraft. The percentage of the population potentially highly annoyed by noise is the only indicator that summarises the impact of all forms of noise pollution. Even in the ESD scenario 25% of the inhabitants of Flanders will still be highly annoyed by noise by the year 2010. A drastic reversal of the current trend is thus required to attain a percentage of less than 20%.
