TI  - Urbanization in China and Changes to the Environment
T2  - URBANIZATION AND PUBLIC HEALTH IN CHINA
M3  - Article
M3  - Book Chapter
AB  - The urbanization level of a country or region is a significant indicator of its level of socio-economic development. Urbanization in China has been gradually gathering pace since the reform and opening up in 1978, and is expected to reach 60% by 2020. Urbanization can promote large-scale industrial and agricultural modernization, along with the development of various services. While China enjoys the dividends of urbanization, it also bears some of its negative impacts. During this process, many environmental issues, such as air, water and soil pollution, the heat island effect and noise pollution in cities have become apparent. This chapter reviews the process of urbanization and the status of several main types of pollution in China with reference to health. It then provides relevant suggestions in order to facilitate a sustainable urbanization process.
